    homepageStatement: "This visual database presents twenty artworks that examine how disability was represented, lived, and interpreted in Western art from the late Middle Ages through the early modern period. Drawn from museums, archives, and libraries across Europe and North America, the collection ranges from sacred healing miracles and civic hospital scenes to court portraiture, street economy, natural history drawing, and artists who worked with and through physical difference.\n\nRather than treating disability as a fixed medical category, the entries consider how images shaped—and were shaped by—religious belief, charitable institutions, court culture, labour, and sensory knowledge. Some works dramatize cure and intercession; others document signed communication, tactile looking, adaptive craft, or the everyday presence of impaired bodies in public life. Short catalogue essays accompany each image, offering historical context alongside questions raised by contemporary disability studies.\n\nThe project is developed at Hong Kong Baptist University as a teaching resource linked to an immersive VR gallery. Students may browse the collection here in a browser, then encounter the same works in virtual corridors designed for Meta Quest headsets. Final project and About copy will replace this placeholder statement when provided by Angelo Lo Conte.\n\n[Placeholder — final homepage statement (~250–300 words) pending from Angelo.]",
