      url: "https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/ep/original/DP-26505-001.jpg",
      permissionsNote: null,
      shortSummary: "Doménikos Theotokópoulos (1541–1614), known as El Greco, was born in Candia, Crete (then under Venetian rule). In the sixteenth century, Crete had a thriving painters’ guild with over two hundred members. El Greco trained in the local post-Byzantine icon tradition and was reco...",
      essay: "Doménikos Theotokópoulos (1541–1614), known as El Greco, was born in Candia, Crete (then under Venetian rule). In the sixteenth century, Crete had a thriving painters’ guild with over two hundred members. El Greco trained in the local post-Byzantine icon tradition and was recorded as a master of the guild in 1563 at the age of twenty-two. He subsequently left for Italy, was deeply influenced by Titian in Venice, and later moved to Rome, where his controversial dismissal of Michelangelo’s Last Judgment alienated local artists. In 1577 he settled in Toledo, Spain, producing his best-known works over nearly forty years.\n\nChrist Healing the Blind is the largest of three extant versions of this subject painted by the artist; the others are in Dresden and Parma. The composition synthetizes three Gospel narratives (Matthew 9:27–31, 20:29–34; John 9:1–14). One blind man kneels before Christ, who anoints his eyes with his fingers, while the other, standing behind him, raises his hand to his own face as he suddenly discovers his regained sight. The tight, forward-facing arrangement of the foreground figures creates a theatrical intimacy, set against a stylized, monumental architectural backdrop that recedes into a dramatic sky.\n\nIn the early modern period, the concept of spiritual salvation frequently overlapped with the restoration of physical health. Physical impairment was often understood as a consequence of sin, and healing therefore signified the remission of sin through Christ’s intercession. Such imagery presented the cure of disability as contingent upon ecclesiastical authority, effectively binding those with impairments to the redemptive power of the Church.\n\nEl Greco's composition renders the blind men as passive recipients of divine grace. Contemporary scholarship questions this premise, recognising impairment as a form of human variation rather than a deficit requiring external correction. The painting's theological framing reveals less about the lived realities of blind individuals in the sixteenth century and more about the Church's claim to mediate between human frailty and salvation.",
