      permissionsNote: null,
      shortSummary: "Fernando del Rincón served at the court of the Spanish Catholic Monarchs Isabella I of Castile and Ferdinand II of Aragon. His work exemplifies the Hispano-Flemish style that dominated Castilian painting at the turn of the 16th century, a manner defined by its meticulous rende...",
      essay: "Fernando del Rincón served at the court of the Spanish Catholic Monarchs Isabella I of Castile and Ferdinand II of Aragon. His work exemplifies the Hispano-Flemish style that dominated Castilian painting at the turn of the 16th century, a manner defined by its meticulous rendering of luxurious textiles, rigid figural poses, and a deep integration of sacred iconography within elaborate, courtly interiors.\n\nThe Miracle of the Black Leg dramatizes the hagiographical legend of Saints Cosmas and Damian, twin brothers and physician martyrs who practiced medicine without charging a fee. According to Jacobus da Voragine’s Golden Legend, the saints miraculously amputated the gangrenous leg of a white deacon and replaced it with the leg of a recently deceased Black man, whom they retrieved from a nearby cemetery. Del Rincón situates the event within a richly appointed domestic chamber. Gilded damask wall hangings frame the interior, while an alcove in the background displays a tondo of the Madonna and Child. On the left, Saint Cosmas holds a ceramic ointment jar and spatula, while Saint Damian attends to the sleeping patient. The deacon reclines beneath a black-and-gold brocade blanket, completely serene, clutching a palm branch. The painting’s visual tension lies in the foreground: the supine Black man lies on the floor, wrapped in white cloth, his anatomical sacrifice made explicit by the missing limb. The transplanted leg is rendered with striking realism, complete with surgical incisions and distinct stitching lines across the upper thigh, while an observant servant crouching in the lower right corner observes the supernatural event.\n\nIn the religious milieu of early modern Spain, the miracle of the black leg served as a potent reflection of Christ's intercessory power through his saints, affirming that physical affliction could be resolved solely through ecclesiastical intervention. Contemporary readings, however, deconstruct the painting's embedded racial and somatic hierarchies. The anonymous Black man is reduced to a passive source of anatomical salvage, deployed to restore the physical integrity, and by extension the spiritual sanctity, of a white Christian patron. Ultimately, the panel tells us less about the actual surgical capabilities of the early Renaissance and more about the theological desire for bodily wholeness, inadvertently revealing the social stratification of the period.",
