      shortSummary: "Domenico di Bartolo (1400–1446) was one of the most innovative painters of the early Renaissance Sienese school. First recorded in 1420 on the payroll of the Siena Cathedral workshop, likely as a young apprentice, he quickly rose to the rank of master painter and was listed in...",
      essay: "Domenico di Bartolo (1400–1446) was one of the most innovative painters of the early Renaissance Sienese school. First recorded in 1420 on the payroll of the Siena Cathedral workshop, likely as a young apprentice, he quickly rose to the rank of master painter and was listed in the local painters’ guild by 1428. Between 1440 and 1444, he was commissioned to execute six of the ten monumental frescoes decorating the Pellegrinaio (Pilgrims’ reception hall) of the Hospital of Santa Maria della Scala, one of the oldest large-scale hospitals in Europe.\n\nThe Care of the Sick is the cycle’s most renowned scene, celebrated for its vivid, secular naturalism and sophisticated use of linear perspective. The work depicts in detail the daily activities carried out in the hospital at the time, offering a remarkably accurate, everyday view of early modern healthcare. In the foreground, a nude patient sits on a wooden stool, having his leg washed by an orderly before further treatment. To the left, a bedridden man is attended to, while nearby a physician holds a glass flask to examine a patient’s urine—a standard diagnostic method of the era. On the far right, a black-robed friar leans in to hear the confession of a dying patient, underscoring the integral role of spiritual comfort alongside physical medicine. Scattered throughout the busy ward are the hospital’s staff, rectors identifiable by their formal black hoods, and even the institution’s resident animals—a dog and a cat tussling near the central basin—grounding the scene in unidealized reality.\n\nSanta Maria della Scala was a major charitable institution dedicated to orphans, pilgrims, and the destitute. Other frescoes within the hospital, also painted by Domenico di Bartolo, illustrate these charitable endeavours. One depicts the institution’s reception of pilgrims, many of whom were sick or injured, alongside the distribution of alms by orderlies. Another fresco portrays the hospital’s care for orphaned girls, depicting nurses, infants, and girls of different ages. Ultimately, this fresco functions as a civic declaration of Siena's virtuous governance, portraying charity as a highly organized, collective, and compassionate social system.",
