      url: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carracci_-_A_hunch_back_boy.jpg",
      permissionsNote: null,
      shortSummary: "Annibale Carracci (1560–1609) was a central figure of the Bolognese school and a foundational artist of the Italian Baroque. With his brother Agostino and cousin Ludovico, he rejected late Mannerist artifice in favour of direct observation from life, establishing a new standar...",
      essay: "Annibale Carracci (1560–1609) was a central figure of the Bolognese school and a foundational artist of the Italian Baroque. With his brother Agostino and cousin Ludovico, he rejected late Mannerist artifice in favour of direct observation from life, establishing a new standard for naturalism. In 1582, the three artists co-founded the Accademia degli Incamminati, one of Europe’s earliest art academies. The academy was radical for its time: it prioritized the study of nature over stylistic conventions, required the study of theoretical subjects including anatomy, and encouraged life drawing from nude models. As part of this practice, Annibale and his collaborators produced drawings of everyday individuals, capturing their physical characteristics with unidealized precision and empathetic restraint.\n\nPortrait of a Hunchback Boy presents a young boy from the waist up, seated and facing right. He is shirtless, draped with a loosely sketched cloth at his waist, and holds his head slightly lowered. The artist records distinct physical features: a pronounced hump on the upper back resulting from a spinal curvature, and a visible swelling in the neck indicative of a goitre. The sketch demonstrates Carracci’s rejection of an idealized representation of the body in favour of natural anatomical asymmetry, in alignment with the ideals that informed his academy. To the right of the figure, the Italian inscription reads “Non so se Dio m’aiuta” [I don't know if God helps me], written in a fine, cursive hand. The first-person phrasing suggests that these were the boy’s own words, transcribed by the artist.\n\nThe inscription may offer an entry point into the sitter’s emotional and spiritual state. In early modern Italy, the phrase “se Dio mi aiuta” was commonly used to express reliance on divine providence, a grace mediated through the Catholic Church. Connecting the boy's recorded words to this theological framework, the drawing implies that physical difference could be situated within a spiritual order in which salvation remained accessible through ecclesiastical intercession. At the same time, the inscription serves as a biographical note that adds a layer of pathos and individuality to the study.\n\nCarracci’s drawing reflects his broader commitment to capturing the full spectrum of human anatomy. It does not frame the boy as a grotesque caricature or an object of pity but records observable, relatively common physical conditions with empirical fidelity, while also registering the subjective presence of the sitter through his recorded words.",
