      id: 5,
      title: "Madonna of the People",
      artist: "Federico Barocci",
      year: "1575–1579",
      medium: "Oil on panel",
      dimensions: "395 x 252 cm",
      location: "Uffizi, Florence",
      url: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Federico_Barocci_-_Madonna_del_Popolo_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg",
      permissionsNote: null,
      shortSummary: "Federico Barocci (1535–1612) was a prominent painter active during the late Italian Renaissance. Following initial training with his father, he entered the workshop of Battista Franco Veneziano. At the age of twenty-five, he received a papal commission to execute frescoes in t...",
      essay: "Federico Barocci (1535–1612) was a prominent painter active during the late Italian Renaissance. Following initial training with his father, he entered the workshop of Battista Franco Veneziano. At the age of twenty-five, he received a papal commission to execute frescoes in the Casino of Pius IV and the Vatican Loggias at the Belvedere. In 1575, Barocci was entrusted with creating an altarpiece for the church of Santa Maria della Pieve in Arezzo, a commission originally intended for Giorgio Vasari, who had died the previous year. A contract dated June 18, 1575, survives alongside extensive correspondence and preparatory drawings documenting the altarpiece’s preparation.\n\nThe resulting altarpiece, the Madonna of the People depicts the compassionate intercession of the Virgin Mary for humanity. The work is divided into two distinct registers. In the celestial upper half, Christ and the Virgin Mary sit upon clouds surrounded by childlike angels; Mary gestures with an open palm, interceding for the diverse assembly of figures below. The lower earthly register presents a densely packed assembly of lay figures embodying various states of physical and material vulnerability. A partially clad man kneels in the foreground, representing the act of clothing the naked; a woman carrying a basket signifies feeding the hungry. In the middle ground, a man wears a sling supporting an injured arm, while, at the lower left, a seated musician plays a stringed instrument, his eyes cast down in a pose suggesting blindness. Interspersed among these figures are wealthy citizens dressed in elaborate fabrics representing Arezzo’s wealthy elite.\n\nBarocci does not segregate these conditions; rather, he integrates them within a continuous architectural space. The injured man, the blind musician, the hungry, and the wealthy patrons all occupy the same visual plane, their proximity emphasizing the shared humanity across social ranks. The painting does not isolate any of these figures for moralizing spectacle. Instead, Barocci presents a cross-section of early modern lay society in which physical impairment, poverty, and economic hardship coexist alongside prosperity as routine aspects of human experience. The painting frames physical and sensory impairment not as an extraordinary crisis, but as inherent dimensions of the human condition placed under the collective intercession of the Virgin. The composition thus functions as a portrait of the entire parish community, diverse in its needs and means, yet unified by a common reliance on divine grace.\n\nII",
