      id: 6,
      title: "Cutting the Stone",
      artist: "Hieronymus Bosch",
      year: "ca. 1494",
      medium: "Oil on oak panel",
      dimensions: "48.5 x 34.5 cm",
      location: "Museo del Prado, Madrid",
      url: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cutting_the_Stone_(Bosch).jpg",
      permissionsNote: null,
      shortSummary: "Hieronymus Bosch (c. 1450–1516) was a Dutch painter from the Duchy of Brabant and a seminal figure of the Early Netherlandish school. His surviving oeuvre is renowned for its fantastical and at times nightmarish imagery.",
      essay: "Hieronymus Bosch (c. 1450–1516) was a Dutch painter from the Duchy of Brabant and a seminal figure of the Early Netherlandish school. His surviving oeuvre is renowned for its fantastical and at times nightmarish imagery.\n\nPopular tradition in the early modern Netherlands suggested that folly, or madness, was caused by a physical stone lodged within the head and could only be remedied through surgical removal. This concept was widely disseminated through contemporary vernacular literature, most notably Sebastian Brant’s Ship of Fools (1494), which allegorized human vice and irrationality. Although the extent of this belief is difficult to ascertain, depictions of the procedure, known as trepanation, were common among Flemish painters.\n\nIn Cutting the Stone, an elderly patient leans back in a wooden chair, bound by white cloth around his abdomen, his shoes tucked beneath the seat. Behind him, the surgeon makes an incision in the man's forehead with a scalpel. The practitioner wears an upside-down metal funnel as a hat: a traditional symbol of foolishness that reveals him as a charlatan or fraudster. A stoneware jug hangs from his belt, while to his right, a monk holding a silver pitcher and a woman balancing a closed book on her head observe the procedure. Notably, a waterlily emerges from the incision rather than a stone, while a second waterlily rests upon the table. The circular panel is framed by a black border with decorative gold ribbons and Gothic inscriptions: “Meester snijt die key ras” [Master, rid me of this stone soon] at the top, and “Myne name Is lubbert das” [My name is Lubbert Das] at the bottom. The name has sparked scholarly debate: Charles de Tolnay interpreted it as a \"gelded badger\" alluding to indolence, while Dirk Bax argued the name held connotations of a simpleton.\n\nBosch’s painting literalizes the premodern belief that cognitive affliction was a tangible physical object embedded in the skull, to be cured through trepanation. The patient is rendered passive, physically restrained, and stripped of his footwear, emphasizing his submission to the invasive procedure. By revealing that the extraction yields a flower rather than a stone, Bosch satirizes the exploitation of cognitive difference. The panel reveals how intellectual vulnerability was stigmatized and leveraged, reducing afflicted individuals to passive subjects of a painful and ineffective intervention.",
      image: "assets/06.jpg",
