      id: 7,
      title: "The Blind Leading the Blind",
      artist: "Pieter Bruegel the Elder",
      year: "1568",
      medium: "Tempera on canvas",
      dimensions: "86 x 154 cm",
      location: "Museo Nazionale di Capodimonte, Naples",
      url: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pieter_Bruegel_the_Elder_(1568)_The_Blind_Leading_the_Blind.jpg#/media/File:Pieter_Bruegel_the_Elder_(1568)_The_Blind_Leading_the_Blind.jpg",
      permissionsNote: null,
      shortSummary: "Pieter Bruegel the Elder (c. 1525–1569) was a key figure of the Dutch and Flemish Renaissance. He joined the Antwerp Guild of Saint Luke in 1551, having trained under Pieter Coecke van Aelst. After travelling in Italy between 1554 and 1555, he spent almost a decade in Antwerp ...",
      essay: "Pieter Bruegel the Elder (c. 1525–1569) was a key figure of the Dutch and Flemish Renaissance. He joined the Antwerp Guild of Saint Luke in 1551, having trained under Pieter Coecke van Aelst. After travelling in Italy between 1554 and 1555, he spent almost a decade in Antwerp before settling in Brussels after marrying Coecke’s daughter Mayken.\n\nPainted in 1568, The Blind Leading the Blind illustrates the biblical parable from Matthew 15:14, in which blind guides lead blind individuals into a pit. The composition features six men moving in a diagonal line across a rustic Flemish landscape. The leading man has tumbled backward into a stream, initiating a chain reaction; the second man cries out mid-fall, pulling on the staff of the third. The three remaining men at the rear have yet to lose their balance, though all grip the shoulders or staffs of the man in front, implying that they, too, will likely tumble into the stream. Beyond biblical connotations, the painting also carries an allegorical meaning. Bruegel often satirised society through visual metaphor, drawing on popular tradition and literary themes. In 1500, Erasmus of Rotterdam published the collection of Roman and Greek proverbs Adagia, featuring the Horatian phrase “caecus caeco dux” [the blind leader of the blind]. Familiar with Erasmus’s work, Bruegel translates the proverb into a pictorial form, transforming the parable into a broader satire on incompetent leadership in the turbulent sixteenth-century Low Countries.\n\nThe distinct ocular pathologies of the men, ranging from opaque corneas to enucleated sockets, reveal Bruegel’s meticulous observation. These differences have allowed retrospective medical diagnoses, most notably by Jean-Martin Charcot and Paul Richer. However, the painting does not individualize the men for medical spectacle; rather, their blindness binds them into a rigidly dependent system. In early modern thought, visual perception was often conflated with the rational faculty. Hence, the absence of sight was not merely a physical impairment, but an allegorical marker for a lack of reason or moral understanding. Bruegel’s depiction confronts this prejudice. Dressed in coarse peasant clothing, his figures are bound together in mutual dependency. Bruegel does not mock their physical condition but rather uses their impending fall to expose the material and spiritual challenges blind individuals faced in the early modern period.",
      image: "assets/07.jpg",
