      id: 8,
      title: "The Clubfoot",
      artist: "Jusepe de Ribera",
      year: "1642",
      medium: "Oil on canvas",
      dimensions: "164 x 94 cm",
      location: "Louvre Museum, Paris",
      url: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_patizambo,_por_Jos%C3%A9_de_Ribera.jpg",
      permissionsNote: null,
      shortSummary: "Jusepe de Ribera (1591–1652) was one of Spain’s most prominent Baroque painters, often mentioned alongside contemporaries such as Diego Velázquez and Bartolomé Esteban Murillo. He spent most of his career in Naples, then under Spanish rule. His works are characterised by stark...",
      essay: "Jusepe de Ribera (1591–1652) was one of Spain’s most prominent Baroque painters, often mentioned alongside contemporaries such as Diego Velázquez and Bartolomé Esteban Murillo. He spent most of his career in Naples, then under Spanish rule. His works are characterised by stark realism and a dramatic use of chiaroscuro inspired by Caravaggio.\n\nPainted in 1642, The Clubfoot portrays a young boy from a low viewpoint near ankle level, positioning him against a low horizon and a cloudy Mediterranean sky. This perspective monumentalizes his figure while drawing attention to his impairment, caused by the inward rotation of the right foot. A wooden crutch, nearly as tall as the boy himself, rests over his shoulder. In his left hand, he holds a parchment inscribed in Latin that reads “DA MIHI ELEMOSINAM PROPTER AMOREM DEI” [Give me alms for the love of God].\n\nRibera’s depiction does not frame the boy as a figure to be viewed with pity, nor does he indulge in over-dramatization. Standing barefoot with a slight, toothy smile, the boy meets the viewer’s gaze with remarkable directness. The inscription clearly establishes his reliance on public charity. In early modern Europe, almsgiving functioned as a reciprocal social practice: a religious obligation for the giver and a necessary survival mechanism for the recipient. Yet, beggars occupied an ambivalent social position, often viewed as both deserving recipients of Christian charity and potential burdens on the civic economy.\n\nBy framing the boy with such direct, unidealized realism, Ribera offers an unfiltered portrayal of early modern life. In this period, responses to impairment were rarely determined by impairment alone; rather, they were shaped by an intersection of social factors, including class, gender, age, and religion. A child born with an impairment into a humble household might have struggled to secure employment and potentially faced a life of mendicancy. In contrast, an individual from a wealthy background could have accessed specialised education and pursued a profession regardless of their physical impairment. The crutch and the alms-seeking note are therefore not universal signifiers of impairment, but specific visual markers of the structural poverty that often accompanied disability in the lower social strata.",
      image: "assets/08.jpg",
