      url: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vel%C3%A1zquez_-_El_buf%C3%B3n_don_Diego_de_Acedo,_el_Primo_(Museo_del_Prado,_c._1644).jpg",
      permissionsNote: null,
      shortSummary: "Diego Velázquez (1599–1660) was the leading painter of the Spanish Golden Age. Born in Seville, he trained under the painter Francisco Pacheco before moving to Madrid in 1623, where he was rapidly appointed court painter to King Philip IV. His output encompassed mythological n...",
      essay: "Diego Velázquez (1599–1660) was the leading painter of the Spanish Golden Age. Born in Seville, he trained under the painter Francisco Pacheco before moving to Madrid in 1623, where he was rapidly appointed court painter to King Philip IV. His output encompassed mythological narratives, religious scenes, and still lifes, but he is best known for his portraits of the royal family and court staff. Portrait of Don Diego de Acedo is part of a series of portraits of court jesters and entertainers, which provided Velázquez with greater painterly freedom than to the formal constraints of royal portraiture.\n\nDon Diego de Acedo was a man with dwarfism who served at the court of Philip IV. He is documented at court from 1635 until his death on 22 October 1660. His administrative role within the court secretariat involved handling the royal signature and overseeing audiences, petitions, and the pardoning process, placing him in close proximity to the king. The painting places de Acedo in a desolate, foggy, mountainous landscape painted in browns and greys. He wears elegant black attire, a stiff white collar, and a large black hat perched at an angle. His hands rest on a heavy tome that fills his lap, surrounded by several additional books and an inkwell beside him. The scale of these objects emphasizes his short stature while simultaneously suggesting his intellectual capacity.\n\nThe painting was first recorded as “Buffoon with Books” in the inventory of the Royal Alcázar Palace in Madrid as early as 1686. It was transferred to the Torre de la Parada (the king’s hunting lodge) in 1701. It was not until the nineteenth century that the portrait entered the Museo del Prado, where the painter and writer Pedro de Madrazo definitively identified the subject as Don Diego de Acedo in 1872.\n\nDespite the earlier description of the sitter as a “buffoon”, court records confirm that de Acedo held a significant administrative position at the Spanish court. Velázquez's portrait visually aligns with this documented role, steering away from the reductive label of a jester. The painting documents that physical variation was not inherently incompatible with holding a literate bureaucratic position at the Spanish court in the early modern period.",
