      id: 13,
      title: "A Book Peddler",
      artist: "Anonymous Artist (Master of the Canesso Peddler)",
      year: "ca. 1670–1690",
      medium: "Oil on canvas",
      dimensions: "170 x 103 cm",
      location: "Metropolitan Museum, New York",
      url: "https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/910384",
      permissionsNote: null,
      shortSummary: "The Master of the Canesso Peddler is the conventional name given to an anonymous North Italian painter active in the late seventeenth century. Influenced by the Caravaggisti, followers of Caravaggio, he worked in a style characterized by stark, unidealized observation and dire...",
      essay: "The Master of the Canesso Peddler is the conventional name given to an anonymous North Italian painter active in the late seventeenth century. Influenced by the Caravaggisti, followers of Caravaggio, he worked in a style characterized by stark, unidealized observation and direct engagement with working-class subjects. The present painting is the work from which the master takes his name.\n\nThe painting depicts a peddler of cheap prints and pamphlets standing outdoors, leaning against a grey stone pillar. His short stature, evident in relation to the pillar and the staff he carries, suggest a form of dwarfism. He wears a large, wide-brimmed black hat, a dark jacket with a white undershirt, and a white apron. His knee-length breeches appear second-hand, likely belonging to a taller individual; discoloured patches at the knees indicate significant wear and repair. His heavy wooden clogs are similarly ill-fitting. In his right hand, he holds a stack of printed sheets; the top one features a woodcut illustration and visible text. A wicker basket hangs from his left arm, while his left hand grips a wooden staff used to support his weight against the pillar. A piece of paper fixed to the pillar above serves as an advertisement for his wares. The background features a cloudy sky, trees, and a rural landscape.\n\nIn early modern Italy, itinerant peddlers fulfilled a vital role in disseminating printed materials—religious tracts, woodcut images, and folk literature—to the broader populace. Here, the book peddler is not depicted as a marginalized or pitiable figure, but as an active participant in the street economy. His wares are clearly displayed, and his posture implies a momentary pause in his daily labours. The painting documents the presence of physical variation within early modern labour networks. Unlike courtly depictions of dwarfs as entertainers or curiosities, this painting presents an individual performing a specific economic function. Physical difference is integrated into the composition without sensationalism; his stature is a fact of his appearance, while his identity is primarily defined by his trade. The staff functions as a practical aid for walking and balance, rather than as a sign of dependency. The details of his worn, ill-fitting clothing further suggest that the artist depicted a real individual rather than an idealised subject. The work thus reflects an early modern social reality in which individuals with physical differences could hold independent economic roles.",
      image: "assets/13.jpg",
